    "collected_at": "2026-11-14T16:19:13.286768+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/14/tesla-cybercab-autonomy-subscription-agreement-terms/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/14/tesla-cybercab-autonomy-subscription-agreement-terms/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-11-14T13:33:00+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-14T13:33:00+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-14T16:20:10.670735+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-14T16:20:10.670735+00:00",
    "body": "Tesla has posted the agreement that comes with its $29,990 Cybercab, and it’s even worse than the order page suggested. The two-seater can’t move without a $299 monthly “Autonomy Subscription” that Tesla can reprice with a month’s notice, suspend for a week, or cut off entirely.\nAnd Tesla wants consumers to sign it while still refusing to give unsupervised self-driving to the Model Y owners it has been selling “Full Self-Driving” to for nearly a decade.\nWhat Cybercab owners agree to\nThe “Cybercab Autonomy Subscription Agreement” (version 20261112) went up on Tesla’s configurator late Friday, hours after the company started inviting select customers to order the Cybercab for personal use at $29,990 plus a mandatory $299 per month.\nThe key line is short: “Your Cybercab will not operate without an active Autonomy Subscription.” There’s no steering wheel and no pedals, so that isn’t a figure of speech. Stop paying and you have a $30,000 object in your garage.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nHere’s what else is in there:\nTesla can change the subscription price with one month’s notice. You pay $29,990 up front, and the car’s usefulness then depends on a monthly fee Tesla sets.\nThe Cybercab only works inside Tesla-enabled Robotaxi geofences. Driving outside them is prohibited and can be technically blocked, and Tesla “may expand, reduce, relocate, or otherwise modify the operating area at any time.”\nTesla can suspend or cancel the subscription for “unauthorized or inappropriate use.” Three or more “strikeouts” for improper or unsafe use trigger a one-week suspension of autonomous driving, during which the car may not operate. Your payment isn’t prorated.\nTesla can remotely access, locate, restrict, or control the car, place it in a “safe state,” or end your access, and the agreement says those actions don’t give you remedies beyond what’s in the terms.\nTesla collects “Autonomy Data,” including image data from the cabin camera above the rear-view mirror, kept for 30 days by default.\nIt’s for personal use only. You can’t put your Cybercab on Tesla’s Robotaxi network, Uber, a delivery app, or a rental platform without Tesla’s written authorization.\nNo wraps, tint, or wheel changes except through Tesla or an authorized provider, or your subscription can be suspended.\nThat personal-use clause kills the whole pitch. For years, Musk told people they’d buy a Tesla, add it to the fleet, and make money while they sleep. The first Tesla sold to consumers for unsupervised operation is contractually barred from doing exactly that.\nThen there’s the geofence. Tesla’s Robotaxi service areas are already smaller in practice than advertised: when we tested them, real coverage was between 59% and 78% of the advertised maps , and parts of the Houston map have been called “fake” by the person tracking it . Now Tesla reserves the right to shrink that area after you’ve paid for the car.\nMark Valorian, who went through the terms on X , summed it up as “an endless variable-rate lease with a ridiculous down payment.” That’s about right.\nOver five years, the car plus 59 paid months of subscription comes to $47,631 before insurance, registration, charging, and taxes, all of which are on the owner.\nTesla says the Model Y runs the same thing\nThis is the part that should make every Tesla owner angry.\nTesla has spent the last year telling everyone that its Model Y is just as much a robotaxi as the Cybercab. Ahead of the Robotaxi launch in Austin, Musk said the cars were “the Model Ys we make currently, there’s no change to it.” On Tesla’s Q3 2026 earnings call last month, AI chief Ashok Elluswamy said Cybercabs and Model Y robotaxis already run the same software, and that customer cars might join the network around Q2 2027 . The Cybercab launches on the same AI4 computer found in the current Model Y.\nSo the hardware is the same, the software is the same, and unsupervised Model Ys are already operating in the exact geofences where Cybercab owners will be allowed to ride.\nYet the people who paid for it get nothing. Tesla started selling “Full Self-Driving Capability” in 2016 on the promise that every car it built had the hardware for it. Owners paid up to $15,000 for the package. In February, Tesla stopped selling it outright and moved to a $99 per month subscription, which still requires you to supervise the car at all times.\nTesla’s answer to all those owners is a new car. One with two seats, no controls, a geofence it can move whenever it wants, and a subscription that costs three times as much as FSD.\nElectrek’s Take\nI’ll say it plainly: this car is for suckers who want to give their money to Elon Musk.\nYou don’t own a Cybercab in any meaningful sense. You pay $30,000 for the right to keep paying Tesla $299 a month, at a price Tesla can raise, in an area Tesla can shrink, under rules Tesla enforces, with a camera pointed at you the whole time.\nTop comment by neilw\nLiked by 4 people\nSo what happens the first time Tesla emails Cybercab owners a month’s notice that the price is going to $399?\nNot to worry. If there's one thing you can count on in this life it's that Tesla will keep it's pricing stable steady for the long-term.\nView all comments\nAnd the Model Y part is what gives the game away. If Tesla truly believed its unsupervised system was ready for customers, the obvious move would be to turn it on for the Model Y owners in Austin who already paid for it. It’s the same computer and the same software in the same geofence. But that would mean honoring a promise made in 2016, on cars already sold, with no new revenue attached.\nA new vehicle wipes that slate clean. No legacy FSD buyers, no HW3 baggage, just a fresh customer locked into a $299 monthly fee from day one. What Tesla built is a subscription with a $30,000 entry fee attached to it.\nSo what happens the first time Tesla emails Cybercab owners a month’s notice that the price is going to $399?\nIf you’re buying an EV, powering it with home solar can cut your fuel costs to nearly zero, with no subscription required. With electricity rates up almost 10% last year and expected to keep climbing, going solar is one of the best ways to protect yourself against rising costs. And with lease and PPA options, you can do it with zero upfront cost and start saving immediately. If you want to find the best deal, check out EnergySage . It’s a free service with hundreds of pre-vetted installers competing for your business, so you save 20 to 30% compared to going it alone. No sales calls until you pick an installer. Get your free quotes here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
